Z04-P13 | Screen watershed land use before collecting surface water | EPA contaminants and MDH drinking water; new manuscript
objective: Evaluate upstream drainage and land use to avoid hidden chemical and microbial pollution before filling a raw vessel.
applies: Selecting a lake, river, creek or spring in rural, forested or mixed-use watersheds. Visual clarity never confirms chemical safety.
equipment: Topographic or regional map, local advisories if available and dedicated raw-water collection container.
action: Trace the drainage upstream on your map and by observation for roads, farms, pastures, mines, industrial sites, storm drains and septic outlets. @W22,W23,ED
action: Prefer smaller headwater streams or protected watershed supplies over downstream channels collecting agricultural or urban runoff. @W22,W23,W09
action: Treat any collected surface water from an acceptable watershed as microbiologically raw until a validated treatment finishes. @W09,W04
failure: Filling bottles immediately downstream of a highway culvert, cattle crossing or fertilized field because the water looks clear. @W22,W09,ED
verify: Name what lies upstream of your collection point and confirm no suspected chemical or sewage discharge enters the channel. @W22,W23,ED
stop: Reject the source if upstream mining, fuel spills, pesticide spraying, sewage or industrial discharge is suspected; seek an alternate supply. @W01,W22
diagram: Check upstream watershed | Exclude industrial and farm runoff | Treat remaining microbial risk
visual: Environmental watershed diagram mapping upstream runoff sources, buffer terrain and chemical exclusion zones. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Drinking-water safety and watershed hydrology

Z04-P14 | Reject water near harmful algal blooms and blue-green scum | CDC, EPA and Minnesota PCA HAB guidance; new manuscript
objective: Recognize harmful algal bloom risks and avoid boiling or drinking cyanotoxin-contaminated water.
applies: Warm, slow-moving lakes, ponds, reservoirs and backwaters during late spring through autumn, especially downwind shorelines.
equipment: Carried safe water, local lake or utility advisories and alternative drinking supply.
action: Inspect the water surface and downwind shore for green, blue-green, turquoise or paint-like scum, surface mats, streaks or foul odor. @W15,W17
action: Keep people and pets out of contact with suspect bloom water and switch immediately to bottled or another verified safe supply. @W15,W17
action: Obey all local algal-toxin advisories even if the water looks clearer after wind shifts; dissolved cyanotoxins can persist without visible scum. @W16,W07
failure: Trying to strain or boil blue-green lake water; heat does not destroy cyanotoxins and evaporation concentrates the toxins. @W15,W16
verify: Confirm the chosen supply has no visible bloom signs and is outside any active harmful algal bloom advisory. @W15,W16,W17
stop: Never boil, bleach or use standard portable filters to drink from a suspected harmful algal bloom; obtain an alternate potable source. @W15,W16,W01
diagram: Spot bloom or advisory | Reject boiling and filters | Use alternate potable source
visual: Environmental shoreline schematic illustrating downwind cyanobacteria accumulation and toxin persistence beyond visible scum. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Environmental health and drinking-water toxicology

Z04-P15 | Separate visual clarity from biological and chemical safety | CDC backcountry water and EPA regulations; new manuscript
objective: Distinguish visible turbidity from invisible pathogens and dissolved chemical contaminants when evaluating water.
applies: Any untreated surface water, rain, snowmelt, spring or well source in backcountry or disaster settings.
equipment: Source assessment checklist, validated microbial treatment and known-safe fallback supply.
action: Treat clear appearance, cold temperature and fast flow as non-indicators of safety; bacteria, viruses, cysts and dissolved chemicals are invisible. @W09,W22,W24
action: Screen for chemical, fuel, heavy-metal, nitrate and algal-toxin hazards first because ordinary field treatments cannot remove them. @W01,W15,W22
action: Clarify cloudy water only as a preparatory step before applying an effective microbial barrier matched to expected pathogens. @W08,W04,W02
failure: Drinking untreated clear mountain stream water or treating cloth-strained clear water as ready to drink. @W09,W08
verify: State separately how suspended solids, pathogens and chemical hazards were each ruled out or addressed before drinking. @W01,W08,W09,ED
stop: If chemical safety cannot be reasonably established or microbial treatment is missing, reject the water and use an alternate supply. @W01,W09
diagram: Clarity is not safety | Screen chemical hazards | Match microbial treatment
visual: Comparative configuration contrasting suspended turbidity, invisible microbes and dissolved chemicals against treatment reach. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Drinking-water safety and public health

Z04-P16 | Assess moving streams versus stagnant ponds and pools | CDC backcountry water and USGS turbidity; new manuscript
objective: Compare physical, biological and sediment trade-offs between flowing streams and standing water bodies.
applies: Selecting a raw backcountry water collection point after chemical and algal-bloom screening.
equipment: Raw-water scoop or vessel, pre-filter cloth and validated downstream microbial treatment.
action: Prefer steadily moving, oxygenated channels away from shoreline debris over stagnant warm pools that concentrate organics, silt and algae. @W24,W16,ED
action: Recognize that moving streams still carry Giardia, Cryptosporidium, bacteria and storm-driven turbidity from upstream wildlife and soil erosion. @W09,W19,W20,W24
action: If only standing pond or lake water is available, inspect carefully for algal blooms, draw gently without stirring bottom mud and pre-clarify before treatment. @W15,W08,W24
failure: Assuming fast-flowing cold streams are sterile or dipping straight into warm stagnant margins that clog filters and harbor cyanotoxins. @W09,W15,W24
verify: Confirm no chemical or algal hazards exist and identify the exact clarification and microbial treatment steps matched to the chosen source. @W01,W15,W08,ED
stop: Reject stagnant water with algal scum, dead animals, oily sheen or foul chemical odor; relocate to a safer source or seek assistance. @W01,W15,W17
diagram: Compare flow and organics | Draw away from bottom mud | Clarify and treat all raw water
visual: Comparative configuration showing flow, sediment load, algal risk and treatment prerequisites across moving streams and standing ponds. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Drinking-water safety and field hydrology

Z04-P17 | Screen springs, seeps and groundwater outlets for hidden runoff | EPA contaminants and CDC backcountry water; new manuscript
objective: Evaluate natural springs and hillside seeps without assuming groundwater emergence guarantees purity.
applies: Collecting water from rocky seeps, piped springs or shallow ground outlets in backcountry or rural terrain.
equipment: Clean scoop or raw bottle, pre-filter cloth and validated microbial treatment.
action: Inspect the area directly above the spring outlet for trails, campsites, livestock, latrines, roads or surface runoff entering the seep. @W22,W09,ED
action: Collect where water emerges cleanly before it pools in mud or vegetation, avoiding any corroded pipe or unknown catchment box of uncertain history. @W09,W06,ED
action: Treat spring and seep water as raw unless an agency confirms current potable testing; shallow groundwater can carry pathogens and dissolved minerals. @W09,W23
failure: Assuming a cold roadside pipe or forest seep is naturally filtered free of Giardia, bacteria or agricultural nitrates. @W09,W19,W23
verify: Confirm upstream ground is clear of obvious waste or spills and record the microbial treatment applied before drinking. @W09,W22,ED
stop: Reject springs near mine tailings, oil sheen, septic drainfields or agricultural chemical runoff; microbial treatment cannot remove dissolved toxins. @W01,W22,W23
diagram: Inspect ground above seep | Collect at clean emergence | Apply microbial treatment
visual: Cutaway view of a hillside spring separating surface-runoff infiltration pathways from the emergence point and raw-water vessel. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Hydrogeology and drinking-water safety

Z04-P18 | Recognize agricultural, livestock and road-runoff contamination | EPA regulations and Minnesota MDH contaminants; new manuscript
objective: Identify landscape markers of nitrate, pesticide, salt, fuel and manure runoff that rule out field water sources.
applies: Rural, suburban, farmland and roadside drainages, especially during spring snowmelt or after heavy rainfall.
equipment: Area map, carried potable water and alternate supply plan.
action: Look for tile drains, ditch culverts, feedlots, heavily grazed banks, road salt residue, sheen or freshly applied fields draining into the channel. @W22,W23
action: Recognize that nitrates, pesticides, road de-icers and petroleum hydrocarbons dissolve in clear water and pass straight through boiling and standard field filters. @W01,W22,W23
action: Move upstream of road and agricultural inputs or rely on carried potable water rather than attempting to treat contaminated ditch runoff. @W01,W22,ED
failure: Boiling roadside ditch water or filtering pasture-runoff creeks and assuming chemical fertilizers or fuel residues are removed. @W01,W22
verify: Confirm the selected water source sits clear of visible road culverts, feedlots and agricultural drainage inflows. @W22,W23,ED
stop: Any fuel sheen, chemical odor, industrial outfall or active agricultural drainage is an immediate rejection boundary requiring another supply. @W01,W22
diagram: Spot farm and road drains | Reject dissolved chemical risk | Move upstream or use alternate
visual: Environmental relationship schematic tracing road salt, fuel, pesticide and manure runoff paths into lower drainage channels. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Environmental health and drinking-water toxicology

Z04-P19 | Assess floodwater and post-storm surface sources | CDC emergency water and NWS flood guidance; new manuscript
objective: Avoid acute chemical, sewage and structural hazards when storm runoff transforms ordinary waterways.
applies: Creeks, rivers, lakes and standing water during or immediately after heavy storms, flash floods or urban flooding.
equipment: Bottled or stored potable reserve, local water advisories and safe high-ground access.
action: Stay out of active floodwater; storm surges carry raw sewage, fuel, submerged debris, agricultural chemicals and extreme turbidity that defeat routine filters. @W01,W24,T07
action: Use stored potable water, sealed commercial supplies or official distribution sites while flood conditions and utility advisories persist. @W06,W07,W14
action: Wait for official public-health clearance or safely accessible, non-flooded headwater sources before resuming routine backcountry water collection. @W07,W09,ED
failure: Trying to filter muddy floodwater at a swollen riverbank, risking both chemical exposure and bank collapse or drowning. @W01,W24,T07
verify: Confirm all drinking water comes from verified potable reserves or cleared sources outside flood-impacted channels. @W06,W07,ED
stop: Never wade into moving floodwater to reach a tap or stream, and never boil floodwater suspected of fuel or chemical contamination. @W01,T07
diagram: Flood mixes sewage and fuel | Rely on safe reserves | Wait for cleared sources
visual: Failure-mode illustration showing how floodwaters combine physical drowning risk, chemical dilution failure and rapid filter clogging. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Emergency management and drinking-water safety

Z04-P20 | Protect against contaminated private wells after flooding | CDC private-well flood guidance; new manuscript
objective: Prevent waterborne illness and electrical hazards when a private well is submerged or affected by floodwater.
applies: Rural homes, cabins and camps supplied by private wells after local flooding or surface-water intrusion around the wellhead.
equipment: Bottled or verified safe water, local health department contact and qualified well-contractor information.
action: Stop drinking tap water from any private well whose casing, pit or pump area was submerged or surrounded by floodwater; use bottled water instead. @W18,W06
action: Watch for chemical, fuel or pesticide spills near the well; if chemical contamination is suspected, do not boil or use the water and call health authorities. @W18,W01
action: Stay clear of flooded electrical pump panels and arrange a qualified well contractor and certified water test before returning the well to drinking service. @W18,ED
failure: Drinking clear tap water from a flooded well after surface water recedes, or attempting DIY shock chlorination around damaged wiring. @W18,W09
verify: Confirm household drinking, cooking and hygiene rely on verified safe water until certified lab tests clear the well. @W18,W29,ED
stop: Do not resume drinking from a flood-impacted private well until professional inspection, disinfection if appropriate and laboratory testing confirm safety. @W18
diagram: Floodwater enters wellhead | Switch to safe supply | Professional inspection and test
visual: Cutaway view of a private well casing showing floodwater intrusion pathways, pump electrical hazard and sanitary isolation boundary. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Drinking-water utility and environmental health

Z04-P21 | Estimate group hydration needs and reserve margins before travel | CDC emergency supply and NPS hiking safety; new manuscript
objective: Plan carried water capacity and refill checkpoints so the group does not run dry in uncertain watersheds.
applies: Day hikes, overnight trips and household preparedness across warm, cold and arid or frozen seasons.
equipment: Leak-proof potable containers, route map marking reliable water sources and weather forecast.
action: Use the CDC household floor of at least 1 gallon per person per day for basic emergency storage, adjusting upward for heat, exertion, pregnancy, illness or pets. @W14,E08
action: Map verified water refill points along backcountry routes and check seasonal status; streams can dry up in late summer or freeze solid in winter. @E01,W09,W25,ED
action: Carry enough verified potable water to reach the next reliable checkpoint plus a delay reserve, along with a working treatment method matched to the route. @W09,W26,ED
failure: Starting a hot or strenuous route with minimal carried water while assuming a seasonal creek shown on an old map will still be flowing. @W14,E08,ED
verify: Confirm total group liters carried, next reliable refill point and backup plan if that source is dry or contaminated. @W14,W09,ED
stop: Turn back early when carried water drops to the return-trip reserve and no verified refill source is confirmed ahead. @E01,W09,ED
diagram: Baseline daily need | Adjust for heat and effort | Map verified refill checkpoints
visual: Comparative configuration showing household reserve baselines versus increased field hydration demand under heat, exertion and cold dry air. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Drinking-water safety and wilderness medicine

Z04-P22 | Select safe food-contact surfaces for rain catchment | B003 rain catchment and CDC rainwater guidance; new manuscript
objective: Minimize chemical leaching and debris pickup when setting up a clean temporary rain-catchment sheet.
applies: Emergency or backcountry rain collection where lawful and no confirmed potable source is reachable.
equipment: Clean food-contact tarp or sheet with known prior use, clean cordage and dedicated raw-water collection vessel.
action: Choose a clean, uncoated or food-contact catchment material with known history; reject tarps stained with fuel, pesticides, antifreeze or heavy mold. @W03,W01,W30
action: Suspend the catchment above ground splash and clear of overhanging branches, bird perches and roof eaves that shed droppings or chemical residues. @W03,W30
action: Direct the lowest corner into a covered raw-water vessel and keep all catchment parts strictly separate from finished drinking containers. @W03,W06,W30
failure: Catching rain off a painted vehicle hood, asphalt roof or dirty groundsheet and drinking it untreated. @W03,W01,W30
verify: Confirm the catchment surface is clean, elevated from splash and drains exclusively into a clearly marked raw container. @W30,W06,ED
stop: Reject collected rain if chemical residue, fuel odor or heavy bird-dropping contamination is present; treat all acceptable rain before drinking. @W01,W03,W30
diagram: Clean elevated sheet | Avoid roof and splash | Drain to raw vessel then treat
visual: Cutaway view of a suspended rain-catchment sheet showing ground-splash clearance, drip funnel and raw-to-clean treatment break. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Drinking-water safety and environmental health

Z04-P23 | Separate first-flush debris and roof runoff from raw storage | B003 P02 and CDC rainwater guidance; new manuscript
objective: Reduce coarse dust, pollen and droppings entering raw rain storage without mistaking screening for purification.
applies: Temporary tarp catchment or emergency roof-downspout collection when no safer potable source is available.
equipment: Clean catchment surface, coarse debris screen, discard bucket and separate raw-water storage vessel.
action: Allow initial rainfall to wash surface dust and loose grit off the catchment onto the ground before diverting flow into the raw vessel. @W30,W03
action: Place a clean mesh or cloth screen over the raw vessel mouth to exclude leaves and insects while keeping screen surfaces on the raw side. @W30,W08,ED
action: Treat roof-collected rain as high-risk raw water because roofing shingles, solder, gutters and bird waste can introduce both pathogens and chemicals. @W03,W01,W30
failure: Assuming first-flush diversion or a leaf screen produces drinkable rainwater without microbial treatment or chemical screening. @W03,W30,W08
verify: Confirm initial washout was discarded, raw storage stays covered and a validated microbial treatment is ready before drinking. @W30,W02,W04,ED
stop: Prefer commercial or verified potable water over roof runoff whenever available; reject runoff from treated, flaking or chemically contaminated roofs. @W03,W01
diagram: Discard initial washout | Screen coarse raw debris | Treat before clean storage
visual: Cutaway vessel diagram illustrating first-flush diversion, coarse raw-side screening and the mandatory downstream microbial barrier. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Drinking-water safety and environmental health

Z04-P24 | Recognize contamination limits of leaf and branch runoff | B003 leaf runoff and CDC rainwater guidance; new manuscript
objective: Identify botanical, chemical and microbial hazards that limit collecting runoff from foliage or tree trunks.
applies: Wet forest environments where rainwater drips from branches or leaves and readers consider improvised plant collection.
equipment: Dedicated clean catchment sheet if available, raw collection cup and validated microbial treatment.
action: Prefer a clean synthetic or food-contact sheet over tree bark or foliage, which accumulate bird droppings, insect frass, pollen, mold and natural plant tannins. @W03,W30,ED
action: Never collect runoff from unidentified plants, toxic shrubs, sprayed roadside trees or sap-oozing bark because dissolved plant compounds or pesticides can enter the water. @W01,W22,W30,ED
action: If clean foliage dew or drip is ever gathered in an extreme emergency from known safe non-toxic surfaces, classify it strictly as raw water requiring clarification and microbial treatment. @W03,W08,W09
failure: Treating clear raindrops gathered off unknown leaves or moss-covered bark as pure drinking water. @W03,W09,W30
verify: Confirm collection surfaces are known non-toxic and chemical-free, and apply full microbial treatment before consumption. @W01,W03,W09,ED
stop: Reject foliage or bark runoff whenever plant identity, roadside spraying or bird-waste contamination is uncertain; use a clean catchment or alternate supply. @W01,W03,W30
diagram: Bark and leaf contaminants | Risk of toxins and droppings | Prefer clean sheet and treat
visual: Material cross-section showing surface dust, tannins, bird droppings and pesticide residues carried off leaves into raw drip water. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Drinking-water safety and botany safety

Z04-P25 | Collect from shallow streams without disturbing bottom sediment | B009 collection and USGS turbidity; new manuscript
objective: Draw clearer raw water from shallow or silty streams so downstream filters and disinfectants work reliably.
applies: Shallow creeks, muddy banks or slow brooks after upstream watershed and chemical screening passes.
equipment: Clean dedicated raw-water scoop or cup, pre-filter cloth and raw-water container.
action: Position yourself on stable footing slightly downstream of your collection spot so foot movement does not push mud into the intake. @W24,ED
action: Skim gently near the upper-middle water layer with a clean raw scoop rather than plunging the bottle mouth into bottom silt or surface scum. @W24,W08,ED
action: Pour through a clean pre-filter cloth into the raw holding vessel if visible grit remains, keeping raw scoop and cloth isolated from clean gear. @W08,W06,ED
failure: Dipping a filter intake directly into churned streambed silt, plugging the cartridge within minutes and forcing untreated bypass attempts. @W24,W08,ED
verify: Raw water collected is free of coarse bottom sand and ready for settling or direct entry to the rated treatment stage. @W08,W24,ED
stop: If bank footing is unstable, current is swift or heavy storm turbidity overwhelms collection, step back to stable ground and choose another source. @W24,T07,ED
diagram: Stand downstream on firm bank | Skim mid-depth gently | Isolate raw scoop and pre-filter
visual: Mechanism diagram illustrating laminar mid-depth skimming above disturbed benthic silt to minimize particulate load on downstream filters. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Drinking-water safety and field hydrology

Z04-P26 | Screen snowpack layers for windblown dust, tracks and spills | B012 P22 and USGS snowmelt cycle; new manuscript
objective: Select cleaner snow strata before melting while keeping raw snow separate from potable drinking water.
applies: Winter backcountry or storm isolation where melting snow is the fallback raw-water source.
equipment: Clean scoop or pot dedicated to raw snow, stove/pot setup and clean finished-water vessel.
action: Avoid snow near roads, parking areas, snowmobile tracks, fuel canisters, camp latrines, animal droppings or discolored windblown dust crusts. @W01,W25
action: Scrape away the surface crust and gather cleaner consolidated snow from beneath the top layer without digging into soil, pine needles or underlying slush. @W25,W08,ED
action: Recognize that even clean white subsurface snow can contain airborne particles and microbes; always treat meltwater before drinking. @W25,W09,W02
failure: Scooping snow next to a roadside snowbank contaminated with de-icing salt and petroleum spray, or assuming white snow needs no microbial treatment. @W01,W25,W09
verify: Collected snow is free of visible grit, discoloration and fuel odor, and is staged strictly on the raw side for melting and treatment. @W25,W01,ED
stop: Reject snow near fuel spills, vehicle exhaust or discolored algal/chemical patches; eating raw snow also chills the core during cold exposure. @W01,W25,E06
diagram: Discard dirty surface crust | Gather clean middle stratum | Avoid soil and treat meltwater
visual: Material cross-section of a winter snowpack showing surface deposition, cleaner mid-pack snow and bottom organic/soil contamination zone. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Cold-weather operations and drinking-water safety

Z04-P27 | Melt snow safely without scorching vessels or wasting fuel | B012 P22 and NPS water purification; new manuscript
objective: Convert raw snow into liquid water efficiently in a heat-safe pot while keeping melting distinct from microbial treatment.
applies: Cold-weather camp with a rated outdoor stove or permitted fire and a suitable metal cooking vessel.
equipment: Heat-safe metal pot with lid, rated outdoor stove and fuel, starter liquid water if available and sanitized finished container.
action: Start with a small layer of liquid water in the bottom of the pot if available so heat conducts evenly and snow does not vaporize and scorch the dry metal. @W02,W26,ED
action: Add snow gradually in dense handfuls with the lid on to reduce wind cooling and fuel consumption, operating the stove strictly outdoors away from shelters. @C05,C15,W26,ED
action: Once melted, clarify any needles or grit if needed and then bring the water to a full rolling boil (1 minute; 3 minutes above 6,500 feet) or apply another validated treatment. @W02,W08
failure: Packing fluffy dry snow into a dry pot over high flame—burning the metal—or drinking freshly melted snow without boiling or disinfecting. @W02,W09,ED
verify: Liquid meltwater completes a timed rolling boil or validated treatment cycle and transfers into a clean covered vessel. @W02,W06,ED
stop: Never melt snow with a fuel stove inside a tent, vestibule or vehicle; stop and switch to carried reserves if fuel or safe outdoor setup is insufficient. @C05,C16,W26
diagram: Seed pot with starter water | Melt covered outdoors | Complete separate treatment
visual: Mechanism diagram comparing conductive heat transfer in seeded meltwater versus scorching dry snow, with outdoor stove boundary. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Cold-weather operations and portable appliance safety

Z04-P28 | Decide when to melt clear ice versus loose snow | USGS snowmelt and MN DNR ice safety; new manuscript
objective: Weigh fuel savings of solid ice against ice-access drowning hazards and mandatory microbial treatment.
applies: Winter camp where loose snow and safe shoreline or icicle ice fragments are available without stepping onto frozen water bodies.
equipment: Safe land-based access, heat-safe metal pot, outdoor stove and validated microbial treatment.
action: Recognize that dense, bubble-free ice yields far more liquid water per pot volume and requires less stove fuel than fluffy dry snow. @W25,W26,ED
action: Collect ice only from stable land—such as safe broken shoreline pieces or clean ground-accessible blocks—never step onto uncertain lake or river ice to chop water holes. @E12,ED
action: Treat melted ice as microbiologically raw water; freezing slows bacteria and preserves cysts rather than sterilizing the source. @W09,W19,W02
failure: Walking onto thin river ice to reach open water or drinking melted lake ice under the myth that freezing kills germs. @E12,W09,W19
verify: Confirm collection occurred strictly from stable land and that meltwater undergoes boiling or rated microbial treatment before drinking. @E12,W02,ED
stop: If reaching ice requires stepping onto unverified lake or stream ice or beneath hanging roof icicles, stay on safe land and melt clean snow instead. @E12,E13,ED
diagram: Stay on stable land | Dense ice saves stove fuel | Freezing does not kill germs
visual: Comparative configuration contrasting water yield, fuel use and access hazards across loose snow, shoreline ice and unsafe lake ice. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Cold-weather operations and ice safety

Z04-P29 | Account for seasonal drought, low flow and warm-water bloom shifts | USGS water cycle and Minnesota PCA HAB guidance; new manuscript
objective: Adjust water-sourcing plans across spring snowmelt, summer low-water heat and autumn leaf-drop conditions.
applies: Multi-season Upper Midwest and North American backcountry planning and emergency water sourcing.
equipment: Seasonal watershed forecast, topographic map, pre-filter cloth and multi-barrier treatment kit.
action: Expect high turbidity, bank erosion and agricultural runoff during spring snowmelt and heavy rains, requiring pre-clarification and stricter chemical screening. @W25,W24,W22
action: Expect dried headwaters, warm stagnant pools and blue-green algal blooms during mid-to-late summer drought, requiring larger carried water reserves. @W15,W17,W14
action: Expect leaf tannins and organic debris clogging fine filters during autumn and frozen shallow creeks in late fall and winter. @W24,W28,ED
failure: Planning an August or November water route using spring runoff expectations without pre-filters, freeze protection or enough carried water. @W24,W17,W28
verify: Match your carried capacity, pre-filter and treatment backup to the current season's water flow, temperature and bloom risk. @W26,W14,ED
stop: When seasonal drought, blooms or freeze-up eliminate acceptable raw sources along the route, carry full potable reserves or reroute. @W15,W09,ED
diagram: Spring turbid runoff | Summer drought and blooms | Autumn debris and winter freeze
visual: Environmental relationship diagram mapping seasonal changes in turbidity, algal bloom risk, water availability and filter vulnerability. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Field hydrology and drinking-water safety

Z04-P30 | Manage winter freeze-up of surface sources and intake gear | CDC freeze-damage limits and NWS cold safety; new manuscript
objective: Prevent hoses, valves, squeeze bags and filter elements from freezing solid during cold-weather collection.
applies: Backcountry travel when air temperatures approach or fall below freezing (32°F / 0°C).
equipment: Wide-mouth bottles, insulated carry sleeves, chemical or boiling backup and dry storage bag for wet filter elements.
action: Prefer boiling or chemical disinfection matched to cold-water contact times when subfreezing temperatures threaten mechanical filters and pump hoses. @W28,W02,W05,W26
action: Drain excess water from squeeze filters and hoses immediately after use and store wet cartridges inside an insulated inner jacket pocket or sleeping bag to prevent freezing. @W28,E10,ED
action: Recognize that cold water slows chemical disinfectant reactions; check product labels for cold-water extended wait times before drinking. @W05,W12
failure: Leaving a damp hollow-fiber filter in an outside pack pocket overnight below freezing, cracking fibers invisibly and continuing to drink bypassed water. @W28
verify: Confirm every wet filter stayed continuously above freezing or switch immediately to boiling or a verified unaffected backup. @W28,W02,ED
stop: Discard or retire any hollow-fiber or ceramic filter suspected of freezing while wet; never guess whether internal fibers survived intact. @W28
diagram: Subfreezing air hits wet filter | Invisible internal fiber crack | Retire filter and switch to boil
visual: Failure-mode illustration showing ice expansion inside wet filter fibers leading to invisible bypass channels and mandatory retirement. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Cold-weather operations and drinking-water treatment

Z04-P31 | Avoid unsafe stream banks, thin ice and steep water access | MN DNR ice safety and NWS flood guidance; new manuscript
objective: Prevent slips, cold-water immersion and falls when approaching rivers, lakes, ravines or winter shorelines for water.
applies: Collecting raw water along steep embankments, mossy rocks, fast currents, flood-swollen rivers or frozen edges.
equipment: Dedicated weighted scoop or cord-tied raw container if needed, stable footwear and partner spotter.
action: Inspect bank stability, current speed, slippery algae-covered rock and ice shelves before stepping toward the water's edge. @E12,T07,ED
action: Choose a gently sloping, firm gravel or rock access point out of fast current; keep one partner back on solid ground when collection footing is awkward. @E12,T01,ED
action: Stay completely off overhanging snow cornices, shelf ice and undercut mud banks that can collapse into deep or cold water. @E12,T07
failure: Leaning over a fast flooded bank or stepping onto a snowy shoreline shelf to reach open water, causing an immersion emergency. @E12,T07
verify: Confirm solid dry footing and an easy step-back path before dipping the raw vessel. @T01,ED
stop: If the only reachable water requires risking a fall, fast current or uncertain ice, abandon that access point and melt snow or move to a safer site. @E12,T07,ED
diagram: Check bank, ice and current | Use firm shallow access | Never risk immersion for water
visual: Decision tree gating water-edge approach on firm footing, safe current and absence of shelf ice before dipping a raw vessel. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Swiftwater safety, ice safety and search and rescue

Z04-P32 | Match gravity settling time and decanting to turbid water | B009 settling and USGS turbidity; new manuscript
objective: Allow heavy silt and clay to drop out cleanly before pouring clearer raw water into a filter or boiling pot.
applies: Muddy, storm-roiled or silty raw water that has passed chemical and algal-bloom screening.
equipment: Wide, stable raw settling vessel, clean raw dipper or pour lip and separate downstream treatment equipment.
action: Fill the raw settling container and set it undisturbed on level ground in shade so coarse sand and silt sink toward the bottom. @W08,W24
action: Gently decant or ladle the clearer upper layer into the treatment vessel without tilting up the bottom sludge layer. @W08,W24,ED
action: Keep the settling container and ladle strictly on the raw side and follow with full microbial treatment (boiling, certified filtration plus disinfection, or verified UV once clear). @W08,W02,W04,W11
failure: Agitating the settled jar while pouring, carrying heavy grit into a fine filter, or drinking decanted water without microbial treatment. @W08,W24
verify: Decanted water shows visibly reduced sediment while raw sludge stays behind and downstream microbial treatment is clearly staged. @W08,ED
stop: If fine suspended clay will not settle enough for UV or filtration, switch to boiling after cloth straining or locate a clearer acceptable source. @W08,W02,W11
diagram: Rest raw water undisturbed | Decant clearer upper layer | Transfer to microbial treatment
visual: Cutaway vessel view showing settled silt bed, clear supernatant draw-off zone and downstream treatment handoff. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Drinking-water safety and treatment mechanics

Z04-P33 | Pre-filter coarse particles with cloth or coffee filter before treatment | B009 clarification and CDC emergency water; new manuscript
objective: Remove suspended grit, leaves and organic clumps cleanly to protect porous filters and improve disinfection reliability.
applies: Turbid or debris-laden raw water from a chemically acceptable source prior to boiling, filtration or chemical disinfection.
equipment: Clean woven cloth, bandana, paper coffee filter or mesh pre-filter, raw catch vessel and validated microbial treatment.
action: Secure a clean cloth, layered paper towel or coffee filter over the mouth of a dedicated raw container and pour settled raw water through slowly. @W08,W24
action: Replace or rinse clogged pre-filter media away from the clean side rather than squeezing muddy sludge forcefully through the weave. @W08,W24,ED
action: Treat pre-filtered water immediately as still microbiologically unsafe—cloth and paper never stop bacteria, viruses or protozoan cysts. @W08,W09,W19,W20
failure: Squeezing a muddy bandana into a drinking bottle or treating coffee-filtered clear-looking water as potable. @W08,W09
verify: Coarse debris is retained on the raw cloth while the strained water enters a validated microbial treatment step. @W08,ED
stop: Reject cloth pre-filtration as a standalone treatment; if no validated microbial barrier is available after straining, do not treat the water as potable. @W08,W09
diagram: Coarse organic debris | Clean cloth or paper weave | Strained raw water to treatment
visual: Material cross-section illustrating cloth/paper weave trapping visible silt while invisible bacteria, viruses and cysts pass through to treatment. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Drinking-water safety

Z04-P34 | Understand what pore-size filtration removes and what passes through | CDC backcountry treatment and filter standards; new manuscript
objective: Match mechanical filter pore ratings accurately against protozoa, bacteria, viruses and dissolved chemicals.
applies: Choosing or using any portable backcountry or emergency point-of-use water filter.
equipment: Filter specification sheet or label showing absolute pore rating or independent certification (NSF/ANSI 53, 55 or EPA purifier standard).
action: Identify the exact model and treatment claim. Nominal and absolute pore ratings differ. Product certification or test evidence must cover the organism of concern. @W04,W20,W21
action: Ordinary field filters often miss viruses. Follow a verified microbial treatment method for remaining germs. If harmful chemical or toxin contamination is suspected, use another safe source and follow local advice. @W01,W04,W21
action: Confirm whether any activated-carbon stage is strictly for taste/odor or holds independent NSF certification for specific listed chemicals; carbon alone does not disinfect microbes. @W21,W22
failure: Assuming a 0.2-micron hollow-fiber filter stops viruses or removes agricultural nitrates, fuel or heavy metals. @W04,W21,W22
verify: State the filter's exact certified target classes and name the second barrier covering any remaining microbial gaps. @W04,W21,ED
stop: Never rely on an unlabeled, uncertified or improvised sand/charcoal filter for microbial or chemical safety. @W04,W08,W21
diagram: Confirm the exact model claim | Address remaining germs | Use another safe source for suspected chemical contamination
visual: Two distinct barriers for emergency water decisions. Suspected harmful chemicals or toxins require a safe alternate source and local advice.
reviewer: Drinking-water microbiology and treatment engineering

Z04-P35 | Select certified filters for Giardia and Cryptosporidium cysts | CDC Giardia, Cryptosporidium and filter guidance; new manuscript
objective: Choose effective parasite barriers where standard chlorine or iodine disinfection alone is unreliable.
applies: Surface water in wilderness, rural or flood-affected areas where wildlife or livestock can shed protozoan cysts.
equipment: Filter rated for specific cyst reduction or removal, filter of one micron absolute or smaller, or boiling setup.
action: Recognize that Cryptosporidium oocysts strongly resist routine chlorine bleach and iodine doses, and Giardia cysts also resist brief cold-water chlorination. @W19,W20,W10
action: Use boiling or a filter independently certified for cyst reduction or removal, or rated at one micron absolute or smaller for parasite reduction. Look for a cyst-specific claim under NSF/ANSI 53 or 58 where certified. @W20
action: Combine cyst-rated filtration with chemical disinfection when viral risk is also present (such as near human sewage or heavy-use corridors). @W04,W20
failure: Relying solely on standard chlorine drops in cold stream water where Cryptosporidium oocysts survive treatment. @W20,W05
verify: Confirm the filter label explicitly states cyst removal or absolute ≤1 micron rating and that seals and cartridge are intact. @W19,W20,W28,ED
stop: If the filter lacks cyst verification or suffers seal or freeze damage, boil the water instead or switch to a known potable supply. @W02,W20,W28
diagram: Routine chlorine misses Crypto | Verify specific cyst claim | Boil when appropriate
visual: Compares parasite barriers. Neither a standard number by itself nor cyst removal establishes protection against all hazards.
reviewer: Drinking-water microbiology and public health

Z04-P36 | Operate pump, squeeze and gravity filters without cross-contaminating outlets | B009 equipment hygiene and CDC treatment; new manuscript
objective: Keep raw intake hoses, wet dirty bags and hands physically isolated from clean spouts and potable bottles.
applies: Field operation of portable hand-pump, squeeze-pouch or gravity-bag water filters.
equipment: Complete filter assembly with marked raw and clean lines, separate storage bags and clean potable bottle.
action: Lay out the raw intake hose or dirty reservoir on one side and the clean outlet spout and open potable bottle on a separate clean surface. @W04,W06,ED
action: Hold the dirty squeeze bag or pump body without letting raw water drip down the outside of the bottle threads onto the clean nozzle or cap. @W06,ED
action: Store the raw hose and dirty bag inside their own sealed pouch apart from the clean outlet hose and drinking bottles after use. @W06,ED
failure: Letting a wet dirty squeeze pouch drip raw stream water over the rim of the clean bottle while squeezing into the opening. @W06,ED
verify: Confirm the clean outlet never touches raw water, dirty bags, ground soil or unwashed fingers during filtering and packing. @W06,ED
stop: If raw water splashes onto the clean outlet or inside the finished bottle, empty or re-treat the batch and clean the contact surface before drinking. @W06,W04,ED
diagram: Dirty intake / raw bag | Cartridge seal boundary | Protected clean outlet and rim
visual: Exploded component view of a portable filter showing raw intake, cartridge barrier, seal interface and clean-side drip guard. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Drinking-water treatment and field hygiene

Z04-P37 | Protect wet hollow-fiber and ceramic filters from freeze damage | CDC backcountry filter maintenance; new manuscript
objective: Prevent invisible ice-expansion damage inside porous filter elements during cold trips and overnight stops.
applies: Any outing where temperatures may drop near or below freezing while carrying a previously wetted filter.
equipment: Sealable dry bag for the filter element, insulated interior pocket or sleeping bag and backup boiling or chemical method.
action: Read the exact model instructions for draining and storage. A cartridge that looks drained can still contain water. @W28
action: Protect the wetted filter against freezing as the manufacturer directs. Maintain a treatment backup for water within its limits. @W28,W02
action: Run the manufacturer's integrity check where specified (such as air-pressure or ceramic gauge checks) only as directed; never assume a visual glance detects internal cracks. @W28,ED
failure: Stashing a wet filter in a pack's outer mesh pocket at dusk when temperatures dip below 32°F, silently rupturing hollow fibers. @W28
verify: Confirm the filter has never been exposed to freezing temperatures while damp, or switch immediately to boiling. @W28,W02
stop: If a wetted filter might have frozen, stop relying on it and follow the model instructions for replacement or an approved integrity check. Use a supported alternate treatment or carried potable water. @W28,W02
diagram: Residual moisture in pores | Below-freezing exposure | Invisible crack bypasses barrier
visual: Failure-mode illustration linking trapped pore water and subfreezing exposure to silent internal membrane rupture and backup switch. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Drinking-water treatment and cold-weather equipment

Z04-P38 | Backflush and inspect field filter cartridges for flow or seal failure | CDC filter guidance and manufacturer maintenance; new manuscript
objective: Restore slowed flow safely with clean water and detect torn O-rings, loose threads or cracked housings early.
applies: Routine field and post-trip maintenance of reusable hollow-fiber, ceramic or carbon-core portable filters.
equipment: Manufacturer backflush syringe or clean-water port, confirmed clean water, spare O-rings if supplied and product manual.
action: Backflush hollow-fiber filters or gently scrub ceramic elements strictly per manufacturer instructions using only clean treated water—never raw water. @W04,W06,ED
action: Inspect cartridge threads, O-rings, check valves and housing seams for cracks, grit or pinched gaskets that let untreated water slip past the element. @W04,W28,ED
action: Track cumulative volume and filter age; replace carbon or composite cartridges on schedule rather than pushing degraded media beyond rated capacity. @W21,ED
failure: Backflushing a hollow-fiber filter with raw stream water—contaminating the clean side—or forcing a clogged squeeze bag until seams burst. @W06,W04,ED
verify: Confirm steady normal flow rate, seated O-rings, clean backflush water source and intact housing before packing. @W04,W06,ED
stop: If flow drops to a trickle despite clean backflushing, or if water bypasses suddenly at high speed, retire the cartridge and switch to boiling or another method. @W04,W28,W02
diagram: Inspect seals and housing | Clean-water backflush only | Verify normal flow resistance
visual: Exploded component diagram highlighting O-ring seals, cartridge thread seat, clean-side backflush port and cracked-housing bypass points. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Drinking-water treatment and equipment maintenance

Z04-P39 | Disinfect clear water with unscented household chlorine bleach | EPA and CDC emergency disinfection guidance; new manuscript
objective: Apply label-verified household sodium hypochlorite accurately when boiling is unavailable and chemical hazards are excluded.
applies: Emergency drinking-water disinfection using fresh, regular unscented liquid bleach (5%–9% sodium hypochlorite) on chemically acceptable water.
equipment: Fresh unscented 5%–9% liquid bleach with readable label, clean dropper or measuring spoon, pre-filter cloth, timer and clean lidded container.
action: Prefer an approved safe supply or boiling when appropriate. Use bleach only as an emergency option and only if the product and concentration are covered by a current official instruction. @W01,W02,W12
action: Follow local public health directions or one complete current EPA or CDC protocol. Verify the exact formulation, water volume and required pretreatment. Do not combine tables or improvise a rounded dose. @W12
action: Follow the selected protocol for the full dose and contact time. A chlorine smell alone cannot prove treatment, and this process does not reliably address all parasites. @W12,W20
failure: Using aged weak bleach, scented laundry bleach, or drinking immediately without the 30-minute contact wait. @W12,W05
verify: Record the source protocol, product concentration, water volume, exact dose and elapsed time. @W12,ED
stop: Use another supply if the product strength or treatment steps are uncertain, or if a chemical, fuel or algal toxin hazard is suspected. @W01,W12,W15
diagram: Confirm product and concentration | Follow one official table | Record dose and contact time
visual: Protocol-selection schematic. Select the applicable local or agency instructions for the entire procedure.
reviewer: Drinking-water safety and public health

Z04-P40 | Match chlorine dioxide and halogen tablets to temperature and contact time | CDC backcountry treatment; new manuscript
objective: Follow exact manufacturer dose, water-temperature and contact-time tables for portable chemical disinfection products.
applies: Backcountry travel using commercial chlorine dioxide tablets/drops, chlorine tablets or iodine products on chemically acceptable raw water.
equipment: Unexpired commercial disinfectant in original labeled packaging, graduated container, pre-filter cloth and watch or timer.
action: Read the product's active chemistry, expiration date and exact volume rating; chlorine dioxide, chlorine and iodine differ sharply in cyst coverage and wait times. @W05,W10,W20
action: Follow the exact product instructions for water clarity, temperature, volume and contact time. If the product does not claim efficacy for the expected pathogen under those conditions, select a suitable treatment or verified supply. @W04,W05,W20
action: Screen every group member before using iodine—avoid iodine-treated water during pregnancy, with thyroid conditions or with iodine hypersensitivity. @W10
failure: Using a short summer wait time in near-freezing stream water, or using expired blister packs with discolored crumbled tablets. @W05,W10
verify: Record exact product, volume, water clarity, start time and label-required finish time before anyone drinks. @W05,ED
stop: Do not guess doses from unlabeled loose tablets or use chemical disinfection to handle suspected fuel, chemical or algal-toxin contamination. @W01,W05,W15
diagram: Match chemistry and user | Adjust wait for cold water | Complete full contact time
visual: Mechanism diagram illustrating how water clarity, cold temperature and active halogen/chlorine-dioxide chemistry govern required contact time. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Drinking-water safety and wilderness medicine

Z04-P41 | Recognize why boiling and chlorine fail against algal and chemical toxins | CDC, EPA and MDH contaminant guidance; new manuscript
objective: Understand the physical and chemical boundary separating living microbes from heat-stable toxins and dissolved chemicals.
applies: Any water source impacted by harmful algal blooms, fuel spills, mine drainage, agricultural nitrates, heavy metals or industrial releases.
equipment: Local advisories, alternate potable water supply and poison-control or public-health contact information.
action: Boiling or a validated disinfectant can control some or all relevant germs depending on the method. These approaches cannot make water contaminated with harmful chemicals or toxins safe to drink. @W01,W15,W22
action: Heating can concentrate some nonvolatile contaminants. Do not infer that the vapor, condensate or remaining liquid is safe. @W01,W15,W22
action: Switch immediately to sealed commercial water, verified municipal stations or an uncontaminated watershed whenever chemical or algal-toxin hazards are present. @W01,W07,W15
failure: Applying a rolling boil or double chlorine dose to green lake scum or fuel-tainted floodwater in the belief that stronger microbial treatment removes poisons. @W01,W15,W16
verify: Confirm every group member can name two contaminants that boiling makes worse rather than safer. @W15,W22,ED
stop: Suspected chemical, fuel, heavy-metal, radioactive or harmful algal bloom contamination is an absolute stop to field boiling and standard disinfection. @W01,W15,W16,W22
diagram: Microbes: killed by heat | Toxins: survive boiling | Evaporation concentrates poisons
visual: Comparative configuration contrasting pathogen thermal inactivation against heat-stable cyanotoxins and dissolved inorganic/organic chemicals. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Environmental toxicology and drinking-water safety

Z04-P42 | Sequence clarification, filtration and chemical or UV disinfection | CDC multi-barrier backcountry guidance; new manuscript
objective: Combine physical clarification, pore filtration and secondary disinfection in the right order to close single-method gaps.
applies: Untreated surface water where turbidity, parasites, bacteria and viruses may coexist and chemical hazards are excluded.
equipment: Pre-filter cloth or settling vessel, cyst-rated filter and matched chemical disinfectant or UV purifier.
action: Clarify turbid water first by settling or cloth straining so sediment does not clog the fine filter cartridge or block UV light. @W08,W24,W11
action: Pass clarified water through a cyst- and bacteria-rated filter into a clean vessel to remove protozoa (Giardia, Cryptosporidium) and particulate shielding. @W04,W19,W20
action: Apply the secondary barrier—label-directed chemical disinfection or rated UV cycle—to inactivate viruses and any bacteria not caught by the filter, keeping threads clean. @W04,W05,W11
failure: Running UV or bleach before settling cloudy water, or filtering after disinfection through a dirty cartridge that reintroduces microbes. @W08,W11,W04
verify: Confirm each barrier ran in strict order (screen -> clarify -> filter -> disinfect) with no raw-side contact after the final step. @W04,W08,W06,ED
stop: If any required barrier fails or chemical contamination is suspected, switch to boiling (if chemically safe) or seek an alternate potable source. @W01,W02,W04
diagram: Clarify solids first | Filter cysts and bacteria | Disinfect viruses last
visual: Mechanism diagram sequencing four progressive barriers from raw source screening through clarification, filtration and viral disinfection. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Drinking-water treatment engineering

Z04-P43 | Aerate and cool boiled water cleanly without recontamination | EPA emergency disinfection and CDC storage; new manuscript
objective: Cool boiled water safely and improve flat taste only with sanitized clean-side containers.
applies: Water that has completed a full CDC rolling boil (1 minute; 3 minutes above 6,500 feet) in a clean pot.
equipment: Clean pot lid, two clean sanitized food-grade pouring vessels and clean storage cap.
action: Keep the pot covered while boiling water cools naturally so airborne dust, ash and insects do not enter the clean batch. @W02,W06
action: If flat taste is an issue after cooling, pour the cooled water back and forth between two clean, sanitized containers in clean air, or add a tiny pinch of clean salt per quart if appropriate. @W27,W06
action: Transfer into a labeled, tightly capped potable vessel without touching the inner rim, spout or water surface with unwashed hands or raw utensils. @W06,ED
failure: Cooling an uncovered pot beside a dusty campfire or pouring boiled water through an unwashed raw funnel to aerate it. @W02,W06,W27
verify: Confirm the pot stayed covered during cooling and any aeration vessels were strictly clean-side sanitized. @W02,W27,W06,ED
stop: Skip open-air aeration in dusty, smoky or unsanitary conditions; if raw splashes enter cooled water, re-boil the batch before drinking. @W27,W02,W06
diagram: Cool covered in pot | Optional clean-vessel pour | Seal in labeled potable bottle
visual: Cutaway vessel view showing covered cooling protection, clean-to-clean aeration pour and sealed potable storage boundary. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Drinking-water safety and public health

Z04-P44 | Choose primary and backup treatment methods by route and elevation | NPS water purification and CDC treatment; new manuscript
objective: Pair two complementary treatment methods before departure so cold, altitude, turbidity or gear failure does not leave the group stranded.
applies: Pre-trip water-system planning across summer trails, high-altitude routes, winter camps and emergency kits.
equipment: Primary treatment method (e.g., cyst-rated filter or stove/pot) plus independent backup (e.g., chemical tablets or boiling vessel).
action: Match your primary method to expected water turbidity, temperature, group size and fuel supply—remembering boiling requires 3 minutes above 6,500 feet. @W26,W02,W24
action: Choose a backup with a different failure mode (for example, chemical tablets or boiling to back up a freeze-vulnerable filter or battery-dependent UV wand). @W26,W28,W11
action: Verify that every container, stove fuel canister, battery and disinfectant packet needed for both systems is packed, compatible and tested before leaving home. @W26,E02,ED
failure: Carrying two battery-powered devices in winter cold or relying solely on a hollow-fiber filter on a high-silt glacial or storm-swollen river. @W26,W28,W11
verify: Confirm you can execute your backup treatment completely independently if your primary device breaks, clogs or loses power. @W26,E02,ED
stop: Do not start an extended remote route with only a single untested treatment device and no safe carried water reserve. @W09,W26,E02
diagram: Assess silt, cold and altitude | Select primary method | Pair independent backup
visual: Decision tree matching elevation, temperature and turbidity constraints to non-overlapping primary and backup treatment pairs. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Backcountry water safety and field equipment

Z04-P45 | Clean and sanitize reusable food-grade water storage containers | CDC container sanitization guidance; new manuscript
objective: Prepare safe food-grade containers before filling them with emergency or backcountry drinking water.
applies: Reusable food-grade plastic or metal water jugs, bottles and bladders prior to filling with potable water.
equipment: Dishwashing soap, warm potable water, plain unscented 5%–9% liquid household bleach, measuring teaspoon and clean drain rack.
action: Inspect the container first—use only intact food-grade vessels with tight-fitting lids, never containers that ever held toxic chemicals, cleaners or fuel. @W06,W13
action: Wash the interior, cap and threads thoroughly with dish soap and warm water, then rinse completely with safe water. @W13
action: Sanitize by swirling a CDC bleach solution (1 teaspoon unscented 5%–9% bleach per quart of water) over all inner surfaces for at least 30 seconds, pour out and rinse with safe water or air dry. @W13
failure: Filling unwashed recycled milk jugs, cracked bottles or former chemical containers with emergency drinking water. @W06,W13
verify: Confirm the container is food-grade, odor-free, rinsed clean after sanitizing and sealed promptly with a clean tight cap. @W13,W06,ED
stop: Discard containers with persistent chemical odor, cracked plastic, damaged gaskets or unknown industrial history; washing cannot make them safe. @W06,W13
diagram: Wash with soap and rinse | Sanitize ≥30 s dilute bleach | Rinse with safe water and seal
visual: Exploded component view showing container body, cap threads, gasket seal and the three-stage wash-sanitize-rinse sequence. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Drinking-water safety and public health

Z04-P46 | Reject incompatible, chemical-residue or unrated water vessels | CDC storage containers and B009 vessel limits; new manuscript
objective: Prevent chemical leaching, breakage and heat failure by screening container materials before storage or boiling.
applies: Selecting emergency storage jugs, field canteens and cooking vessels in household or improvised settings.
equipment: Marked food-grade containers (e.g., HDPE/food-safe plastic or food-grade stainless steel) and permanent marker for labeling.
action: Reject any jug, drum or bottle that previously stored fuel, pesticides, motor oil, pool chemicals, paint or industrial cleaners—trace residues cannot be washed out reliably. @W06,W13,W01
action: Avoid glass containers for emergency water storage or transport because breakage causes sudden water loss and injury; reject degraded brittle plastic jugs. @W06,W13
action: Never heat or boil water in lined, painted, soldered, unknown-alloy or plastic containers not expressly rated by the manufacturer for direct heat. @W01,W02,ED
failure: Storing drinking water in a rinsed windshield-washer jug or boiling water inside an epoxy-lined aluminum bottle or unknown salvaged can. @W06,W01,ED
verify: Confirm every storage vessel is food-grade with known clean history and every boiling pot is plain, unlined, heat-rated metal. @W06,W02,ED
stop: When no verified safe vessel exists for boiling or storage, rely on sealed commercial water or seek assistance rather than heating unknown containers. @W01,W06,W09
diagram: Food-grade clean history | Reject prior chemical jugs | Heat only rated plain metal
visual: Comparative configuration contrasting approved food-grade storage and plain metal boil pots against rejected chemical, glass and lined vessels. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Drinking-water safety and materials safety

Z04-P47 | Match boiling vessels to stoves and outdoor fires safely | B009 vessel notes and NPS/CPSC stove safety; new manuscript
objective: Prevent tipping burns, vessel failure and carbon monoxide hazards when heating water for microbial treatment.
applies: Boiling acceptable raw water outdoors on a portable camp stove or authorized campfire ring.
equipment: Plain single-wall heat-safe metal pot with lid and lifter/handle, stable outdoor stove or authorized grate and level non-combustible base.
action: Use only single-wall, unlined heat-safe metal vessels—never heat sealed bottles, vacuum-insulated flasks (which can rupture) or plastic containers over flame. @W02,C15,ED
action: Check pot stability on stove supports or fire grates on level cleared outdoor ground so boiling water cannot tip onto legs or extinguish the burner with spilled fuel. @C15,C02,ED
action: Keep all stove and fire boiling strictly outdoors away from tents, vestibules and vehicles, and use a pot lifter or insulated grip when removing hot lids. @C05,C16,C15
failure: Placing a vacuum-insulated steel bottle on coals or balancing an oversized heavy pot on a narrow ultralight stove inside a tent vestibule. @C05,C15,ED
verify: Confirm single-wall unlined metal construction, unsealed lid venting, level outdoor support and clear space around the hot zone. @C15,C05,ED
stop: If the vessel is sealed, lined, unstable or the only sheltered space is enclosed, stop heating and use an alternate safe method or supply. @C05,C15,W02
diagram: Single-wall unlined metal | Level outdoor stove support | Vent lid and clear hot zone
visual: Exploded view of a safe outdoor boiling assembly contrasting single-wall vented pots against sealed/insulated rupture hazards. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Portable appliance safety and drinking-water safety

Z04-P48 | Prevent bottle, bladder and valve freezing during winter transport | NWS cold safety and CDC cold-weather limits; new manuscript
objective: Keep treated drinking water liquid and accessible during subfreezing travel without damaging bottles or soaking insulation.
applies: Winter hiking, skiing, snowshoeing or cold vehicle emergencies below 32°F (0°C).
equipment: Wide-mouth insulated bottles, tight-sealing leak-proof caps and insulating sleeves or inner-pack placement.
action: Prefer wide-mouth hard bottles over narrow-neck bottles or thin hydration bite-valves and tubes, which freeze shut quickly in subfreezing wind. @E15,E10,ED
action: Carry water inside insulated sleeves or near the center of your pack wrapped in spare layers, storing wide-mouth bottles upside down so ice forms away from the threads first. @E15,ED
action: Check cap gaskets before stowing inside dry insulation or a sleeping bag so a leaky lid cannot soak critical dry clothing or bedding. @E10,W06,ED
failure: Relying on an exposed shoulder-strap hose that freezes solid in the first mile, leaving liters of water inaccessible until camp. @E15,ED
verify: Confirm caps seal leak-free, mouth openings are accessible without frozen valves and water stays liquid at scheduled drink breaks. @W06,ED
stop: Never warm frozen plastic bottles directly over open flame or stove burners; thaw safely in warm water or sheltered body/ambient heat. @W02,C15,ED
diagram: Wide-mouth leak-proof bottle | Insulated inner-pack carry | Avoid exposed freeze-prone tubes
visual: Cutaway view of an insulated wide-mouth bottle stored inverted inside pack layers versus a frozen exposed hydration tube. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Cold-weather operations and field equipment

Z04-P49 | Label and physically separate raw, in-treatment and potable containers | B009 hygiene and CDC storage; new manuscript
objective: Eliminate mix-ups between untreated water, actively disinfecting batches and finished drinking water.
applies: Multi-person camps, households under boil advisories and backcountry groups running batch water treatment.
equipment: Permanent marker or high-contrast tape, distinct raw-only and potable-only vessels and timer or written batch log.
action: Assign unmistakable physical differences—color, shape or bold waterproof labels—to raw collection bags, active chemical-wait vessels and ready-to-drink containers. @W06,W05,ED
action: Keep raw vessels, scoops and pre-filters in a separate physical zone from clean drinking bottles, cups and cooking utensils. @W06,W08,ED
action: Tag any chemically disinfecting bottle with its finish time (or keep its lid visibly marked) so no one drinks before contact time expires. @W05,W12,ED
failure: Handing an unlabeled clear bottle mid-disinfection to a thirsty companion or scooping boiled water with the same mug used to dip stream water. @W06,W05,W09
verify: Any group member can identify raw, waiting and potable vessels immediately in dim light without asking or guessing by clarity. @W06,W09,ED
stop: If a container's treatment status becomes uncertain or raw water contacts a clean rim, reclassify the entire vessel as raw until re-treated. @W06,W09,ED
diagram: Raw zone: marked dirty | Waiting zone: timed batch | Clean zone: sealed potable
visual: Comparative configuration showing three physically separated container zones with high-contrast labels and clean-side dispensing control. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Drinking-water safety and public health

Z04-P50 | Rotate household water reserves and inspect seals and storage heat | CDC emergency water supply; new manuscript
objective: Maintain stored household potable water so containers stay intact, cool and ready when municipal service fails.
applies: Home, cabin and vehicle emergency water reserves prepared ahead of outages, storms or advisories.
equipment: Commercially bottled water or sanitized food-grade jugs, permanent marker, cool dark storage space and calendar reminder.
action: Label self-filled potable containers with fill date and rotate stored tap water every 6 months; follow printed expiration dates on sealed commercial water. @W14,W06
action: Store water in a cool, dark location off bare concrete if recommended and well away from gasoline, pesticides, paint thinners or cleaning chemicals whose vapors can permeate plastics. @W06,W01
action: Inspect stored jugs periodically for leaks, swollen caps, brittle plastic or off-odors, and keep a portable portion ready for rapid evacuation. @W06,W14,T13
failure: Stacking emergency water jugs next to lawnmower gas cans in a hot garage for years without checking seals or rotating water. @W01,W06,W14
verify: Confirm fill/expiration dates are current, seals are tight, storage stays cool and chemical-free, and at least 1 gallon per person per day for 3+ days is accessible. @W14,W06,ED
stop: Replace any leaking, sun-degraded or chemically exposed container immediately rather than trusting its contents during an emergency. @W01,W06
diagram: Date and rotate every 6 mo | Store cool away from fuels | Inspect seals and evacuation grab
visual: Exploded maintenance layout detailing storage environment isolation, six-month rotation tagging, seal inspection and evacuation reserve staging. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Household preparedness and drinking-water safety

Z04-P51 | Enforce potable-water boundaries for hygiene, wounds and infant formula | CDC emergency water and advisories; new manuscript
objective: Apply safe-water standards consistently to teeth brushing, food washing and wound rinsing during advisories, and keep infant formula under its own preparation rules.
applies: Boil-water advisories, backcountry camps and disaster outages where untreated water is nearby for utility tasks.
equipment: Verified potable water, labeled utility water if the notice permits, ready-to-feed formula for formula-fed infants, soap and clean utensils.
action: Use only bottled or properly boiled-and-cooled water for drinking, brushing teeth, washing ready-to-eat produce, making ice and washing food-contact dishes. @W29,W07
action: Treat infant formula as its own task (see Z14-P74): ready-to-feed is safest, especially under 2 months, premature or with a weakened immune system. Powder is not sterile, so safe water alone is not enough; follow current CDC and local directions. Under a chemical advisory, boiling does not help: use bottled water or ready-to-feed. @P26,P36,W07
action: Rinse open wounds only with confirmed clean potable water (or sterile saline if available); never irrigate wounds with untreated stream, flood or bloom-affected water. @W29,W15,T01
action: Under a Do Not Use advisory, obey official prohibitions on bathing, dishwashing and skin contact completely until authorities lift the order. @W07,W29
failure: Brushing teeth or washing lettuce with unboiled advisory tap water, or treating safe water as the only precaution when mixing powder for a newborn. @W29,W07,P26
verify: Confirm every ingestion, oral-hygiene, food-prep and wound-care task draws from the verified potable supply, and that infant formula follows its separate CDC directions. @W29,W07,P26,ED
stop: If safe water cannot be secured for infant formula or wound care, prioritize ready-to-feed liquid formula and request immediate public-health or medical assistance. @P26,W29,T02,ED
diagram: Drinking, teeth, food & ice: potable water | Infant formula: separate rules; ready-to-feed safest | Do-Not-Use: zero tap contact
visual: Task routing: drinking, teeth, food and dish tasks use verified potable water; infant formula follows its own CDC rules; wounds get potable water or saline; a Do Not Use notice stops all tap contact. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Public health and wilderness medicine

Z04-P52 | Recognize filter bypass, expired bleach and incomplete UV cycles | CDC water treatment limits; new manuscript
objective: Catch silent treatment failures before anyone drinks an inadequately treated batch of water.
applies: Field and household operation of portable filters, chemical disinfectants and battery-powered UV purifiers.
equipment: Working backup treatment method, product labels, watch/timer and batch tracking log.
action: Treat sudden unusually fast filter flow, cracked housings, missing O-rings or prior freezing of a wet cartridge as signs of mechanical bypass. @W28,W04
action: Reject unlabeled, expired or contaminated reagents and any batch that did not meet its official time and dose requirements. Odor cannot verify microbial safety. @W05,W12
action: Treat blinking error indicators, low-battery shutdowns, turbid water or oversized bottles as incomplete UV cycles that leave microbes active. @W11
failure: Drinking a batch after a UV wand flickered out mid-cycle or after a frozen squeeze filter suddenly flowed twice as fast as normal. @W11,W28
verify: Confirm each treatment batch met its full mechanical integrity, dose/odor or rated UV cycle criterion before releasing it to the clean side. @W04,W11,W12,ED
stop: Whenever any treatment device shows bypass, power failure or uncertain dose, quarantine the batch and re-treat by boiling (if chemically safe) or switch to another supply. @W02,W11,W28
diagram: Abnormal flow or UV error | Quarantine suspect batch | Re-boil or switch supply
visual: Failure-mode illustration linking filter bypass, degraded bleach and aborted UV cycles to observable cues and immediate batch quarantine. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Drinking-water treatment and equipment safety

Z04-P53 | Recognize chemical odor, sheen, industrial runoff and taste warnings | CDC and EPA chemical water hazards; new manuscript
objective: Identify sensory and environmental warning signs of toxic chemical contamination while recognizing that many chemicals are odorless.
applies: Screening surface water, flood-affected wells, damaged pipes and emergency storage containers before treatment.
equipment: Local advisories, carried potable water and alternate supply contacts.
action: Reject any water showing iridescent fuel sheen, chemical or solvent odor, soapy foam from industrial discharge, strange color or dead fish/wildlife nearby. @W01,W22,W15
action: Recognize the opposite trap: dangerous concentrations of lead, arsenic, nitrates, pesticides and many cyanotoxins have no smell, taste or color—never taste suspect water to test it. @W22,W23,W16
action: Keep suspected chemical-contaminated water off skin and away from cooking pots, filters and clean storage containers to avoid contaminating your equipment. @W01,W06,W18
failure: Dipping a reusable filter into oily storm runoff to see if carbon removes the smell, ruining the cartridge and still risking toxic exposure. @W01,W21,W22
verify: Confirm zero sensory or landscape indicators of chemical release and verify no local Do Not Drink or Do Not Use notice applies. @W01,W07,W22,ED
stop: Any suspected fuel, solvent, pesticide, heavy-metal or algal-toxin hazard requires immediate source abandonment and switching to an alternate safe supply. @W01,W15,W22
diagram: Sheen, odor or runoff cue | Never taste to test | Abandon source and protect gear
visual: Failure-mode illustration separating visible/odor chemical cues from invisible dissolved toxins and showing mandatory gear/source rejection. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Environmental toxicology and drinking-water safety

Z04-P54 | Shift to alternative water supplies when local sources are unusable | CDC advisories and emergency water supply; new manuscript
objective: Execute a structured fallback plan when chemical spills, algal blooms, drought or equipment failure eliminate local water sources.
applies: Household water disruptions, contaminated local lakes/wells and backcountry trips encountering dry or polluted sources.
equipment: Local emergency distribution info, vehicle/exit map, sealed potable reserve and communication device.
action: Inventory remaining sealed commercial water and verified potable household reserves (including safe drained water-heater tanks only if municipal supply was uncontaminated and power/fuel is shut off per official guidance). @W06,W07,W14,ED
action: Locate official municipal bulk-water distribution sites, neighboring uncontaminated utilities or alternate mapped watersheds reachable without creating travel hazards. @W07,W01,E01
action: Reduce physical exertion and heat exposure while conserving safe drinking water; prioritize hydration for infants, older adults and medically vulnerable members. @E08,W14,T10
failure: Trying to push deeper into a dry or bloom-affected backcountry corridor or attempting risky improvised distillation of chemical-contaminated water. @W01,W15,E01
verify: Confirm remaining safe liters, hours of margin and the exact safe route to an official potable refill or exit. @W14,W07,ED
stop: Turn back or relocate early before safe drinking water runs out; never drink chemically contaminated water as a stopgap. @W01,W07,ED
diagram: Audit remaining potable reserve | Cut heat and exertion loss | Relocate to verified supply
visual: Decision tree directing transitions from local source rejection to reserve auditing, exertion reduction and verified alternate potable sources. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Emergency management and drinking-water safety

Z04-P55 | Escalate to emergency assistance when safe hydration cannot be maintained | NPS emergency plan, ARC first aid and CDC water; new manuscript
objective: Recognize severe dehydration or unresolvable water shortage early enough to summon help safely.
applies: Stranded outdoor parties or isolated households without safe water, working treatment or safe self-evacuation routes.
equipment: Phone, emergency communicator or NOAA radio, written location details and shade or thermal shelter.
action: Halt strenuous travel in shade or wind protection to slow fluid loss through sweat and respiration while assessing remaining safe water and exit distance. @E08,E05,W09
action: Watch the group for worsening dizziness, confusion, inability to keep fluids down, minimal dark urination or heat illness—signs that require prompt medical help. @T01,T05,T09
action: Contact 911, local emergency management or search and rescue early with your verified location, party size, water status and any medical or mobility needs. @T02,T06,T10
failure: Waiting until severe confusion or heat stroke develops before calling for help, or drinking fuel- or bloom-tainted water out of panic. @T05,T09,W01,W15
verify: Confirm your location, water shortage duration and any medical symptoms have been communicated clearly to responders or emergency contacts. @T06,T02,ED
stop: Confusion, collapse, severe heat illness or multi-day inability to obtain safe water is an emergency requiring immediate responder activation. @T02,T05,T09,W01
diagram: Halt exertion in shelter | Screen dehydration and heat signs | Call responders early with location
visual: Decision tree connecting unresolvable water shortage and symptom screening to early shelter stabilization and emergency rescue activation. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Wilderness medicine, public health and search and rescue
